High-risk AI systems must technically allow automatic event logging over the system’s lifetime, and both providers and deployers must keep the automatically generated logs they control for at least six months, unless another Union or national rule says otherwise. The regulation also requires providers to give deployers information needed to collect, store and interpret the logs, and in some cases specific log contents must be recorded. [Article 12(1)] [Article 19(1)] [Article 26(6)] [Article 13(3)(f)]

- The system itself must have logging capabilities that automatically record events over its lifetime. [Article 12(1)]
- Those logging capabilities must support traceability for:
  - identifying situations that may lead to a risk or a substantial modification;
  - post-market monitoring;
  - monitoring the operation of certain high-risk AI systems. [Article 12(2)]
- For high-risk AI systems in Annex III, point 1(a), the logs must at minimum record:
  - each use’s start and end date/time;
  - the reference database checked against;
  - the input data that matched;
  - the natural persons involved in verification of results. [Article 12(3)]
- Providers must keep automatically generated logs under their control for a period appropriate to the intended purpose, at least six months, unless Union or national law provides otherwise. [Article 19(1)]
- Deployers must also keep automatically generated logs under their control for a period appropriate to the intended purpose, at least six months, unless Union or national law provides otherwise. [Article 26(6)]
- If the provider or deployer is a financial institution subject to Union financial services law governance requirements, the logs are maintained as part of the documentation kept under that law. [Article 19(2)] [Article 26(6)]
- Providers must include in the instructions for use, where relevant, a description of the mechanisms that allow deployers to properly collect, store and interpret the logs in accordance with Article 12. [Article 13(3)(f)]
